    year = "1986",
    doi = {10.2307/3234910},
    journal = {Polity},
    volume = {19},
    number = {2},
    pages = {194--212},
    note = "CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that Kant taught men to transcend parochial perspectives, proposing perpetual peace achieved through history, with cosmopolitanism necessary for it, while noting the limits human nature imposes (per abstract). RELEVANCE: Earlier Anglophone treatment of the Idea's progress and cosmopolitan aim, useful as a baseline for later revisionist readings (Kleingeld, Lindstedt). POSITION: Interpretation of Kant's progress and cosmopolitanism.",
    keywords = "kant, progress, cosmopolitanism, INCOMPLETE, Low",
    abstract = "Kant taught men to transcend their individual or parochial perspectives and look at themselves as cosmopolitans. He proposed a condition of perpetual peace, achieved through history, as an alternative to the state of nature which Rousseau thought impossible to recover. Cosmopolitanism is necessary for perpetual peace. Professor Nichols notes that Kant is nevertheless aware of the limits human nature imposes on cosmopolitanism. She goes on to argue that his teaching leads men to seek a world order that may be neither possible nor desirable.",
